{"study_accession":"PRJNA958205","secondary_study_accession":"SRP433786","title":"Aberrant non-canonical NF-κB signalling reprograms the epigenome landscape to drive oncogenic transcriptomes in multiple myeloma [RNA2]","description":"In multiple myeloma, abnormal plasma cells establish oncogenic niches within the bone marrow by engaging the NF-κB pathway to nurture their survival while they accumulate pro-proliferative mutations. Under these conditions, many cases eventually develop genetic abnormalities endowing them with constitutive NF-κB activation. Here, we find that sustained NF-κB/p52 levels resulting from such mutations favours the recruitment of enhancers beyond the normal B-cell repertoire. Furthermore, through targeted disruption of p52, we characterise how such enhancers are complicit in the formation of super-enhancers and the establishment of cis-regulatory interactions with myeloma dependencies during constitutive activation of p52. Finally, we functionally validate the pathological impact of these cis-regulatory modules on cell and tumour phenotypes using in vitro and in vivo models, confirming RGS1 as a novel p52-dependent myeloma driver. We conclude that the divergent epigenomic reprogramming enforced by aberrant non-canonical NF-κB signalling potentiates transcriptional programs beneficial for multiple myeloma progression. Overall design: RNA-sequencing (RNA-seq) of CD138 positive plasma cells purified from BM aspirates obtained from multiple myeloma patients.","pubmed":"38514625","geo_series":"","n_samples":7,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN34290758","study_accession":"PRJNA958205","blood_class":"bone_marrow","class_from":"sample","title":"MM10, RNAseq, biorep1","description":"MM10, RNAseq, biorep1","sex":"male","geo_sample":"GSM7211405","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2024-03-24","attributes":[["source_name","Bone marrow"],["tissue","Bone marrow"],["disease","Multiple Myeloma"],["tumor stage","Newly Diagnosed MM"],["cel